What's the difference between `markForCheck()` and `detectChanges()`?

## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

Change detection runs in two layers: Zone.js decides *when* to check (after any async task completes the zone), and Angular's dirty-checking walks the tree and re-evaluates each bound expression by reference/value comparison, not a deep diff. `OnPush` lets a component skip that walk unless its Input reference changed, a local DOM event fired, an async-piped Observable emitted, or someone calls `markForCheck()`/`detectChanges()` — and Signals add a fifth, independent trigger: reading a signal subscribes that view, and writing it marks that view dirty directly, bypassing the Input check entirely.

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

Angular's change detection has three cooperating layers. First, Zone.js patches every async API the browser exposes — `setTimeout`, `addEventListener`, `Promise.then`, XHR, `fetch` — so that whenever one of those fires and the zone's task queue empties out (`onMicrotaskEmpty`), Angular knows a callback just ran that *might* have changed state. Second, on that signal, `ApplicationRef.tick()` walks the entire component tree once, depth-first from the root, and for each component runs its compiled template's update function, comparing each individual bound expression's new value against the value recorded last check using `===`/`Object.is` — this is "dirty checking," and critically it's expression-by-expression, never a deep object diff.

Third, `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` lets a component opt out of being checked on a given pass unless a specific trigger applies to it: its `@Input` reference changed (mutating the object in place does not count — only reference replacement does), a DOM event originated inside its own template (this fires regardless of whether any Input changed at all), an Observable bound via the `async` pipe emitted (because `AsyncPipe` calls `markForCheck()` internally), or something manually calls `markForCheck()`/`detectChanges()`.

The follow-up — why OnPush sometimes updates with no Input change — has two concrete answers I verified directly: first, a click handler bound in the OnPush component's *own* template marks that component dirty purely because the event originated there, completely independent of any Input; I proved this live with a local "+1" button that incremented a local counter and the check count with zero Input touched. Second, Signals add an entirely separate reactive-dependency mechanism: reading `signal()` in a template subscribes that view, and writing the signal — even from a totally different component with zero Input relationship — marks that view dirty directly. I demonstrated this with a shared `CounterStore` signal incremented by a click in the *root* component, which updated a downstream OnPush component that had no `@Input` at all.

## 12. Quick Revision

Zone.js patches async APIs → triggers Angular's tick after zone stabilizes. `tick()` walks the whole tree, checks each bound expression by reference/value, not deep diff. `OnPush` skips a component's check unless: Input reference changed, local template event fired, async-piped Observable emitted, or `markForCheck()`/`detectChanges()` called manually. Mutating an object in place never triggers OnPush; replacing the reference does. Signals add a fifth trigger: reading a signal subscribes the view; writing it marks that view dirty directly, independent of Input identity. Verified live: local click marks OnPush dirty with zero Input change; shared signal write marks a no-Input OnPush component dirty from a different component's click; unrelated setTimeout ticks Default but leaves all OnPush-based components completely untouched.

## 13. Memory Trick

**"Zone knows WHEN, dirty-check knows WHAT, OnPush says NO unless: new ref, local event, async pipe, or manual push — and Signals skip the ref check entirely."**
